Statement of changes (required by the licence above)
-----------------------------------------------------

The software was changed by the maintainers of this fork.

Changes fall into two periods. Until 2026-09-30 nothing outside the
build files was touched, and the statement that no change affected a
reported figure held without qualification. On 2026-09-30, after NIST
reviewed the defects this fork had reported upstream and agreed with
several of them, repairs were made to the estimator sources as well.

Branch policy, and when it changed (recorded 2026-10-01 from the history).
Until 2026-09-30 this fork followed the rule: master stays pinned to
upstream behaviour, each fix lives on its own branch off upstream, and no
fix is merged into master (see the open PR branches fix/bitstring-gate-
word-size #256 and fix/multimmc-binary-overread #268, and the audit commit
cbffcbc which states "master's behaviour and pinned output are unchanged").
That rule was SUPERSEDED on 2026-09-30, deliberately and not by drift: a
separate session installed a canonical repair queue (audits/2026-09-30/
FINDINGS-TRACKER.md) and audits/BUG-REPAIR-GUIDE.md, whose stated policy is
that a defect is resolved only when upstream is corrected OR this fork
contains the correction with a regression test (option B). Under that
policy the repairs below were committed DIRECTLY to master (first estimator
change c2f1dcd/ed88de9, 2026-09-30), not merged from the PR branches.
MASTER IS NOW THE PATCHED BUILD. The supersession was a decision; see the
"Product-dependency status" section below for why patched was chosen over
unpatched.

One inconsistency the history shows, recorded rather than smoothed over:
not every reported fix is on master. The bitstring-gate fix (F01, upstream
#253, PR #256 — gate the bitstring estimators on word_size, not the
observed alphabet) is STILL BRANCH-ONLY: master still reads `alph_size > 2`
(cpp/non_iid_main.cpp), so a two-valued multi-bit source at or above the
1,000,000-sample minimum is still over-credited in the shipped master
build. The sub-minimum intake refusal (c2f1dcd) does not cover it, because
the hazard is a full-size capture. Whether #256 should land on master is an
open item for the independent review; until it does, "master is the patched
build" means the repairs listed below, not #256.

What was observed across the changes below, stated as scope rather than
as a guarantee, because the evidence is a finite set of datasets and
not a proof about every qualifying input:

  * No unintended numerical change was observed on the datasets tested.
    The pinned-output check on bin/ringOsc-nist.bin is unchanged at
    0.12644573619604868, and the reference comparison against
    cpp/selftest/refdata/ differs on exactly the two files it differed
    on before, with the same eight predictor values, for the platform
    reason recorded in upstream issue #155.

  * Two changes alter results deliberately, and are not covered by the
    sentence above:

      - F09 (commit 69b6094) corrects the MultiMMC run of correct
        predictions. Where the defect bit, the MultiMMC estimate rises:
        on the audit's dataset from 0.00211176482535928 to
        0.93188950992564512. Because the reported figure is a minimum
        over the estimators, an input on which MultiMMC was the binding
        minimum will report a HIGHER figure after this change than
        before. The earlier value was wrong, being too low, so the
        higher figure is the correct one, but anyone comparing a report
        made before this change with one made after must expect it. No
        such input was demonstrated: on the audit's own dataset the
        overall figure is unchanged at 0.0008876062538599284 because a
        different estimator binds.

      - N-01 (commit c8799f4) makes a binary chi-square independence
        test with m = 1 fail, as SP 800-90B 5.2.3 requires, where it
        previously passed. That changes an IID verdict rather than a
        min-entropy figure, and it changes it on exactly the datasets
        the standard says it should.

  * bin/ and cpp/selftest/refdata/ are untouched. The reference data is
    NIST's and is not regenerated here.

  * No estimator's formula, cut-off, rounding or confidence bound is
    altered. Most estimator changes only turn producing an unmeasured
    number, reading out of bounds, or aborting the process into
    declining to produce an estimate, which the combination logic
    already handled. The two exceptions are F09 and N-01, described
    above: they change results deliberately. This sentence said "only"
    until 2026-10-01, which those two contradict.

